    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1098">

                <pb facs="1136" n="1126" sameAs="frus:frus1949v09/pg1126"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.801/8–749: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Counselor of Embassy in China</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Jones</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Nanking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-08-07T12:00:00+08:00">August 7,
                            1949—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received August 7—2:22 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1733. Before his departure,<anchor n="54" xml:id="d1098-f1"/> Ambassador learned
                    from reliable source of conversation between General Chen Yi<anchor n="55"
                        xml:id="d1098-f2"/> and Chen Ming-shu<anchor n="56" xml:id="d1098-f3"/> in
                    Shanghai which may offer some explanation Communist allegations Communist China
                    port blockade is of American origin. (See Shanghai telegram 2906, July 23,
                    repeated Nanking 1634.<anchor n="57" xml:id="d1098-f4"/>) Chen Yi said Communist
                    intelligence reported that it was Admiral Badger<anchor n="58" xml:id="d1098-f5"
                    /> who had first suggested idea of blockade to Generalissimo. He added that it
                    was obvious, in light this suggestion, that blockade represented official US
                    policy. Source of report locally was Lo Hai-sha, confidant of Chen Ming-shu and
                    latter’s Nanking representative.</p>
                <p>While we are not inclined give credence to this piece Communist intelligence, it
                    may indicate predilection of CCP<anchor n="59" xml:id="d1098-f6"/> agents for
                    reporting what they think their superiors would like to hear. Likewise possible
                    this allegation will be introduced into CCP propaganda re blockade at opportune
                    moment.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Jones</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1098-f1" n="54" xml:id="d1098-f1-content">
                <p>For departure of the Ambassador from China, see <ref target="frus1949v08">vol.
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">viii</hi>
                    </ref>, “The Embassy in China after occupation of Nanking by Chinese
                    Communists”.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1098-f2" n="55" xml:id="d1098-f2-content">
                <p>Communist mayor of Shanghai.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1098-f3" n="56" xml:id="d1098-f3-content">
                <p>Shanghai representative of the Kuomintang Revolutionary Committee (KmtRC)
                    cooperating with the Communist Party.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1098-f4" n="57" xml:id="d1098-f4-content">
                <p>
                    <ref target="frus1949v08#ch6">Vol. <hi rendition="#small-caps">viii</hi>,
                        “Political and military situation in China”, chapter VI</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1098-f5" n="58" xml:id="d1098-f5-content">
                <p>Vice Adm. Oscar C. Badger, Commander, U.S. Naval Forces in the Western
                    Pacific.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1098-f6" n="59" xml:id="d1098-f6-content">
                <p>Chinese Communist Party.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
